      "text": "Methodologically, this study treats the Qurʾān as a self-referential discourse whose morphology, semantics, and internal cross-references together provide the primary framework for clarifying its key terms and narrative logic—without recourse to external exegetical or historical systems. This linguistic approach does not dismiss the post-Qurʾānic tradition; rather, it adopts a Qurʾān-centric method that first defines its vocabulary (ʾummī, kitāb, ṣuḥuf, qalam) through internal coherence before engaging later commentary. Section 5 elaborates on this framework, situating it within a broader hermeneutical tradition that views linguistic analysis as an act of faithful inquiry rather than polemical revisionism. This pan-textual approach reflects the Qurʾān’s own invitation to examine its internal coherence and consistency (Q 4:82), treating semantic harmony across verses as an interpretive principle rather than subordinating Qurʾānic language to later theological assumptions.",
      "source_kind": "authored_research",
      "evidence_role": "argument_not_scripture",
      "external_discussion": false
