      "text": "وَمِنْهُمْ أُمِّيُّونَ لَا يَعْلَمُونَ الْكِتَابَ إِلَّا أَمَانِيَّ وَإِنْ هُمْ إِلَّا يَظُنُّونَ\n\nWa-min-hum ʾummiyyūn lā yaʿlamūna al-kitāba illā ʾamāniyya wa-in hum illā yaẓunnūn.\n\n“And among them are unlettered ones who do not know the Scripture except in wishful thinking, but they are only assuming.” (Q 2:78)\n\nHere the self-referential Qurʾān itself defines ʾummiyyūn as “those who do not know the Book.” Their deficiency lies in ignorance of revelation, not in the inability to read or write. They replace revealed knowledge with ʾamānī (“wishful thinking”) and ẓann (“assumptions”).\n\nThe word ʾUmmī (أُمِّيّ) is commonly connected to the root hamza mīm mīm (أ م م)— “source, mother, community”—following the fuʿʿī pattern, which denotes belonging. Thus, ʾummī literally means “one from among the people,” or “one of the common, unscriptured folk.”",
