      "text": "The word ummi occurs in the Qur’an six times, four times as a noun (Q 2:78; Q 3:20, Q 3:75; Q 62:2) and twice as an adjective (Q 7:157-158). Let's look at the remaining five occurrences below:\n\nVerse | Phrase / Context | Description and Semantic Function\n\nQ 3:20 | “…wa-qul lilladhīna ūtū al-kitāba wa-al-ʾummiyyīn…” | Contrasts those given the Book (Ahl al-Kitāb) with al-ʾummiyyīn—the unscriptured peoples lacking prior revelation.\n\nQ 3:75 | “…dhālika bi-annahum qālū laysa ʿalaynā fī al-ʾummiyyīna sabīl…” | Explicit mention of al-ʾummiyyīn (“those without prior scripture”); contrasts Ahl al-Kitāb’s in-group ethics toward the ʾummiyyūn, reinforcing that ʾummiyyūn ≠ illiterate, but “non-scriptured others.”\n\nQ 7:157–158 | “…al-nabiyy al-ʾummī…” “…wa-rasūlihi al-nabiyyi al-ʾummiyy…” | Refers to “the Prophet from among the unscriptured”—one raised without earlier scripture who becomes its first bearer.\n\nQ 62:2 | “Huwa alladhī baʿatha fī al-ʾummiyyīn rasūlan minhum…” | Describes a Messenger raised among the unscriptured nation, tasked with reciting, purifying, and teaching the Book.\n\nHence the word ʾummiyyīn is contrasted with the people of the book in the Qur’an. Ahl al-Kitāb = people who possess earlier scripture. ʾUmmiyyūn = people who do not.\n\nThus, when the Qurʾān calls Muhammad al-nabiyy al-ʾummī (Q 7:157), it simply means: “The Prophet from a people without previous revelation.” Before revelation, he shared their ʾummiyyah; afterwards, he became teacher of the Book (Q 62:2).",
