      "text": "This table summarizes representative early Qurʾānic manuscripts across different regions, dates, and preservation states. Coverage percentages reflect surviving material, not original manuscript completeness. Textual characteristics describe correspondence at the consonantal (rasm) level unless otherwise noted.\n\nManuscript | Approx. Date (CE) | Preservation Coverage | Textual Characteristics | Geographic Location\n\nCodex Mashhad (Ḥijāzī) | Probably 7th century; parchment C14 660–772 CE | >90% | Very High Concordance: Standard ʿUthmānic rasm; originally arranged in an alternative surah order later modified. | Mashhad, Iran\n\nṢanʿāʾ Palimpsest —Upper Text (Standard Ḥijāzī) | 7th – early 8th century | Fragmentary (multiple sūrahs, partial verses) | High Concordance: Conforms almost exactly to the standard Qurʾānic rasm. | Ṣanʿāʾ, Yemen (Arabian Peninsula)\n\nṢanʿāʾ 1 Palimpsest—Lower Text (Archaic Ḥijāzī) | Parchment dated 578-669 CE | Fragmentary | Textually divergent: Non-ʿUthmānic textual type with genuine wording differences and alternative surah order. Semantically non-divergent. | Ṣanʿāʾ, Yemen\n\nTopkapı Sarayı Medina 1a (M1) | 8th CE | ~100% (Virtually Complete across 391 folios) | High Concordance: Pristine ʿUthmānic text type throughout. | Medina, Arabian Peninsula",
