      "text": "Before turning to the control example in Q 25:4, it helps to clarify these grammatical categories.\n\nReflexive → The subject performs the action upon or for itself.\n\nExamples:\n\nittakhadha (Q 4:125) = “to take for oneself, to adopt.”\n\nittakhadhū (Q 25:30) = “they took (for themselves).”\n\nCausative → The subject causes another agent to perform the action.\n\nExamples:\n\narsala (Q 13:38) = “to cause to go / to send forth.”\n\naʿlama (Q 2:33) = “to cause someone to know.”\n\nThis distinction is foundational in Arabic morphology: the presence of the prefix ʾa- (Form IV) signals externalization of the act, while the ta-infix of Form VIII signals internalization—action reflected back on the subject. Hence, if the meaning was intended to be “he caused to be written down” the Form IV verb would have been aktaba which is not the case in Q 25:5.",
